From: Chokri Hammedi <52697989+blue0x1@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] loader: run PS5 code on the PS4's CPU (no AVX2, FMA, BMI2)

PS5 games and apps are built for Zen 2 and use AVX2, FMA and BMI2, which the
PS4's Jaguar CPU does not have. When the host lacks any of them, every such
instruction in a loaded module is rewritten at load time to a replacement
built from SSE4.2/AVX/BMI1 (the existing guest instruction patcher places the
jumps and trampolines). Instructions that cannot be rewritten there fault at
run time and are replaced then (with a jump written in place when the site
has 5 bytes or more).

- jaguarCompat.cpp: the replacements. 256-bit integer ops run as two 128-bit
  halves; cross-lane permutes use vperm2f128/vpermilps/vblend; per-element
  shifts go through the stack; FMA in single precision is computed in double
  precision (exact product, the sum rounded twice); VFMADDSUB/VFMSUBADD
  alternate add and subtract; gathers load one element at a time; BMI2 uses
  saved RAX/RCX/RDX and keeps RFLAGS when they are still needed.
- The 128-byte red zone below RSP is never touched.
- KYTY_JAGUAR_COMPAT=1 turns it on on a newer CPU, =0 off, =trap makes every
  such instruction take the run-time path (both for testing).
